===Etymology===
From {{etyl|gem-pro|ang}} {{m|gem-pro|*hwat}}, from {{etyl|ine-pro|ang}} {{m|ine-pro|*kʷos}}, {{m|ine-pro|*kʷod}}. Cognates include Old Frisian ''[[hwet]]'' (West Frisian {{term|wat|lang=fy}}), Old Saxon ''[[hwat]]'' (Low German {{term|wat|lang=nds}}), Dutch {{term|wat|lang=nl}}, Old High German ''[[hwaz]]'' (German {{term|was|lang=de}}), Old Norse ''[[hvat]]'' (Danish {{term|hvad|lang=da}}, Swedish {{term|vad|lang=sv}}), Gothic [[𐍈𐌰]]. The Indo-European root also led to the Latin {{term|quod|lang=la}}, Lithuanian {{term|kàd|lang=lt}}, and Irish {{term|cad|lang=ga}}.

